lican district lean with a nearly 50-to-1 fundraising advantage over Harp, whose campaign shows almost no financial footprint, and every major rater places the seat in the Safe or Solid Republican column. The favorable Democratic environment narrows the expected margin somewhat, but not the likely outcome.", "watch": "Substantial late outside spending against Cammack or unexpected polling showing a single-digit race would be the main trigger for revision.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Kat Cammack (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Florida's 3rd District sits about ten points to the right of the country, and Republican Kat Cammack is seeking a fourth term with the advantages of incumbency. She has raised roughly two million dollars and holds about 877 thousand on hand, while Democratic nominee Seth Harp has raised under forty thousand with about three thousand in the bank. An independent, Mike Klein, is also on the ballot but runs a minimal campaign.\n\nEvery major race rater, including the Cook Political Report, Inside Elections, Sabato's Crystal Ball, and the Economist, places the seat in the Safe or Solid Republican column, and Polymarket prices Cammack's chances near ninety-four percent. No public general-election polls are available, so attention turns to fundamentals and ratings. A Democratic-leaning national environment could narrow the margin, but the district remains safely Republican.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0356, "margin": -14.68}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0357, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0316, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0435, "margin": -12.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0175, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0207, "margin": -14.0}]}